
# THE GATE HISTORY IS APPEND-ONLY FACTS, SO UNION-MERGE IT.
#
# Two branches that each ran Gate B both append a run row to the end of this file, and git's
# default merge sees two different edits to the same place: a conflict, on a file of 500+
# near-identical rows that nobody can resolve by reading. `union` takes BOTH sides instead.
#
# That is safe here only because of how the file is defined (see docs/GATE_HISTORY.md): each line
# is an immutable fact keyed by runId, nothing is ever rewritten, duplicates are dropped on read,
# and the order of lines carries no meaning. Do NOT copy this to a file that stores derived or
# mutable state — union merge on those silently keeps both stale halves.
corpus/gate-history.jsonl merge=union

# THE GATE B COVERAGE MAP IS ONE ARTEFACT, AND THE NEWER ONE WINS.
#
# A session that can fetch the nightly's map commits it onto the branch it tests (gate-covmap.py sync), so two
# branches can carry different maps. The `covmap-newest` driver (installed by .claude/hooks/session-start.sh) keeps the
# one with the later builtUtc. GitHub's own merge button cannot run a driver: merge main locally first. NOT `binary`: that macro is `-diff -merge -text`, and its -merge overrode the driver (measured).
corpus/gate-covmap/methods.json.gz -text -diff merge=covmap-newest

# GATE A'S FINGERPRINT LEDGER IS APPEND-ONLY FACTS TOO (phase 2, tools/gate-a-ledger.py).
#
# One pass per line, keyed by its fingerprint: a duplicate is dropped on read, and a line union brings back after a
# prune is still a true pass (the next fold prunes it again). Two branches that each folded never conflict.
corpus/gate-a-ledger.jsonl merge=union

# THE GATE HISTORY STORE (tools/gate_ledger.py): run files are new paths and never conflict; a month file is
# written by the roll-up, which may run on two branches for one month. Same facts, keyed by runId, deduped on read.
corpus/gate-history/*.jsonl merge=union

# THE BUNDLED CBSCRIPT COMPILER READS ITS OWN RESOURCES LINE BY LINE, SPLIT ON "\n" (parse/Tables.java reads
# parser_tables.txt). A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true gives those files CRLF, and every compile then fails
# ("NumberFormatException: For input string: \"27\r\""), so a Windows source build cannot make a datapack. The release
# jar is built on Linux and never had it. Keep the vendored tree LF on every checkout until moddy-cbscript reads any
# line ending and is re-vendored (tools/refresh-cbscript-bundle.sh); found by the platform smoke (docs/RELEASING.md).
third_party/moddy-cbscript/** text=auto eol=lf
